<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdul Samad of Selangor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sultan Abdul Samad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdul Samad of Selangor was the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Selangor, reigning from 1857 to 1898. His reign saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> rush that drew Chinese miners into the Klang valley and the civil war among Selangor chiefs known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klang-war">Klang War</a>. In 1874 he accepted a British Resident, bringing Selangor under British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdul-samad-of-selangor.jpg" alt="Abdul Samad of Selangor" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lambert &amp; Co., G.R. / Singapore · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KITLV_-_7248_-_Lambert_%26_Co.,_G.R._-_Singapore_-_Sir_Abdul_Samad,_Sultan_of_Selangor_-_circa_1890.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raja Abdul Samad was born in 1804 at Bukit Melawati, the son of Raja Abdullah, younger brother of Sultan Muhammad Shah of Selangor. He strengthened his position by marrying the sultan's daughter Raja Atfah in 1844, became chief of the Selangor valley and held the title Tengku Panglima Raja with authority over Langat. Muhammad Shah died on 6 January 1857 without naming an heir. His legitimate heir, Raja Mahmud, was too young, and his other sons and sons-in-law were ineligible, so the sons-in-law Raja Jumaat and Raja Abdullah, hoping to be the power behind the throne, backed Abdul Samad, who was chosen by consensus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> from the Ampang mines with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straits-settlements">Straits Settlements</a>, and the mines drew more Chinese miners with the help of Raja Abdullah and the Kapitan Yap Ah Loy. In 1866 he gave Raja Abdullah authority over Klang, which deepened Abdullah's feud with Raja Mahadi and led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klang-war">Klang War</a>. The sultan twice appointed his son-in-law Tengku Kudin as vice yamtuan and arbitrator, in 1868 and 1871; Tengku Kudin brought in Pahang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and Sir Andrew Clarke, the first British involvement in local politics.</p><p>After pirate attacks Clarke placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frank-swettenham">Frank Swettenham</a> with the sultan as a live-in advisor in August 1874, and James Guthrie Davidson became the first British Resident of Selangor. Abdul Samad built the Jugra Palace and moved the royal capital to Jugra in 1875, the state capital moved from Klang to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a> in 1880, and in 1893 he helped found the Victoria Institution in Kuala Lumpur. He attended the first durbar of the Federated Malay States at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-kangsar">Kuala Kangsar</a> in 1897, and was known to mingle with his people in markets and at cockfights.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 6 February 1898, aged 93, and was buried in the Sultan Abdul Samad Mausoleum at Bukit Jugra. His heir apparent, Raja Muda Raja Musa, had died in 1884, so the throne passed to Musa's eldest son, Raja Sulaiman Shah. The Sultan Abdul Samad Building and the Sultan Abdul Samad Jamek Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a> are among the places named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abdul Samad of Selangor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sultan Abdul Samad</b> accepted a British resident in 1874.</blockquote><a href="/state/selangor-sultanate">Selangor Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-selangor">Kuala Selangor</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>. Died in Jugra, in what is now <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=3.34,101.25&amp;year=1865" aria-label="Kuala Selangor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7360 2039 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abdul Samad of Selangor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7810 2309h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7810 2309h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=3.34,101.25&amp;year=1865">What was here in 1865? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
